:title("Java (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses Java and executes it directly, without generating code. The
goal is the whole practical language; tests/java-test-full.java is the ratchet that
measures how far it is, and all 22 of its sections pass today.

Types: classes with instance and static fields (with initializers), instance,
static and abstract methods, OVERLOADED constructors with explicit this(...) and
super(...) chaining, static and instance initializer blocks (run in the order they
are written, before the constructor body), single inheritance with dynamic dispatch
and super.m() calls (a class must appear before its subclasses), records (canonical,
compact and secondary constructors, generated accessors and equals, hand written
accessors win), interfaces (constants, abstract methods, default and private
instance methods, static methods, and Iface.super.m() to pick one of two inherited
defaults), anonymous classes over a class or an interface, ENUMS (constructor
arguments, per-constant bodies as anonymous subclasses, values / valueOf /
ordinal / name / compareTo), NESTED, INNER and LOCAL classes (Outer.this and the
qualified o.new Inner(...) creation), and annotation types (parsed and dropped -
nothing reads them at run time).

Values: int / long / double arithmetic on one 32 bit-truncating number type,
boolean, String, arrays, null, and char as a boxed value that renders as its glyph
but compares and calculates as its code, so c == 65, 'b' - 'a' == 1 and
(char)(c + 1) == 'c' all hold. Literals cover hex / binary / octal / underscored /
suffixed integers, fractional, exponent and hex floats, char literals with the full
escape set (including the one-to-three digit octal form), and TEXT BLOCKS with
incidental-indent stripping, trailing-space stripping and the line-joining and
space-preserving escapes.

Statements and expressions: if/else, while, do-while, both for forms, the classic
colon switch and the arrow switch statement, switch EXPRESSIONS with yield in both
forms, labeled break and continue, try/catch/finally with TYPED and MULTI catch
clauses over a real java.lang exception hierarchy (Throwable down to the common
RuntimeExceptions, with getMessage), try-with-resources closing in reverse order,
throw with genuine unwinding, instanceof with a pattern binding, PATTERN SWITCH
(type patterns, case null, when guards and record DECONSTRUCTION patterns, which
instanceof takes too), enum constant labels, VARARGS (the call site collects the
trailing arguments, or forwards an array as-is), multidimensional and jagged
arrays with bare initializers and the C-style declarators, lambdas and method
references (static, bound, unbound and constructor) that become real values of the
functional interface they are assigned to, the full operator set including the
bitwise and shift operators with Java int semantics, and the compound assignments.

UNQUALIFIED calls work - check(x), not just this.check(x) - and pick their
receiver by Java's own rule: a method the enclosing class or an ancestor declares
static is called on the class descriptor, every other one on the enclosing
instance, and a static context has none, so the descriptor stands in there too.

Widened surface (parsed so a whole file is accepted): generics are parsed and
erased, call-site type arguments included; casts are identity except the primitive
ones, which truncate or box; implements / throws / permits clauses, TYPE
ANNOTATIONS in every position JLS 9.7.4 allows, qualified and generic supertypes,
several declarators per field, array class literals and the stray semicolon after
a type declaration are all parsed. -warn-unsupported downgrades what is left to a
warning so call graphs, CFGs and traces still work on partly understood files.

Not genuinely modelled: generic type CHECKING (erased), sealed-hierarchy and
switch EXHAUSTIVENESS checking, method overloading by parameter type (constructors
are picked by arity), the flow scoping of pattern variables (a binding outlives the
test that introduced it), implicit FIELD access (a field is reached via this.x or
ClassName.x, the explicit style many code bases enforce - only calls resolve
unqualified), modules, threads and reflection.

Fields live directly on the instance object, methods on the class descriptor.
main(String[] args) of class Main is the entry point; its return is void, so self
checking programs end with System.exit(failures).") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident-dollar.abnf") ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// A stray ';' after a type declaration (`class F { };`) is legal and means
// nothing, at the top level and inside a class body alike.
Program     = [ Package ] { Import } { UnsupType | ClassDecl | RecordDecl | StraySemi } ;
StraySemi   = ";" ;

// An optional package header (parsed and ignored) then repeated imports. Each
// import path is resolved against the runtime builtins (core.stdlibImports); one
// that does not resolve aborts, unless -warn-imports downgrades it to a warning
// (see resolveImport in interp-core.js). 'import static a.b.C.d' resolves the
// dotted path too. QualName also accepts a trailing '.*' wildcard. Neither Package
// nor Import leaves a value on the stack.
// package-info.java carries its annotations on the package declaration.
Package     <~~ pop() ~~> = LeadMods KwPackage QualName ";" ;
Import      <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~> = KwImport [ "static" KwEnd ] QualName ";" ;
QualName    <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> = Id { "." Id } [ "." "*" ] ;

ClassDecl   <~~ buildClass(takeAll()) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] KwClass Id <~~ push(up.in); jEnterClass(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ]
              [ KwExtends ExtRef ]
              [ ImplementsSkip ] [ Permits ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// A supertype may be qualified and generic (`extends Outer.Super`, `extends
// A<B>.C`). The type arguments are dropped and the dotted name is kept whole -
// jResolveClass tries it, then falls back to the last segment.
ExtRef      <~~ push({ext: jJoinDots(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] } ;

// record Pair(int a, int b) { } -- generates fields, a constructor and accessors.
RecordDecl  <~~ buildRecord(takeAll()) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] KwRecord Id <~~ push(up.in); jEnterClass(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] RParams
              [ ImplementsSkip ] [ Permits ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
RParams     <~~ push({rparams: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } ] ")" ;

// A member starting with '{' (or 'static {') can only be an initializer block, so
// both come first and no other member alternative is disturbed.
Member      = StaticInit | InstInit | NestedType | Ctor | AbstractMethod | Method
            | CompactCtor | Field | StraySemi ;
// static { ... } runs once when the class is built; { ... } runs on every
// construction, in TEXTUAL order with the field initializers around it.
StaticInit  <~~ push({kind: "sinit", body: pop()}) ~~>
            = "static" KwEnd Block ;
InstInit    <~~ push({kind: "iinit", body: pop()}) ~~>
            = Block ;

// A constructor. Its body is NOT a plain Block: an explicit this(...) / super(...)
// invocation may only stand first, and it decides what initializes the object, so it
// is parsed as a separate head rather than as an ordinary statement.
Ctor        <~~ push(makeCtor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] [ SkipAngle ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ Throws ] CtorBody ;
CtorBody    <~~ push(makeCtorBody(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" [ CtorCall ] { Statement } "}" ;
// A record's COMPACT constructor: no parameter list at all - it receives the record
// components, normalizes them, and the canonical assignment happens afterwards.
CompactCtor <~~ push(makeCompactCtor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Block ;
// An abstract (or interface) method: a signature and no body.
AbstractMethod <~~ push({kind: "amethod", name: pop()}) ~~>
            = Mods [ SkipAngle ] Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ Throws ] ";" ;

Method      <~~ push(makeMethod(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods [ SkipAngle ] Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ Throws ] Block ;

// One `int a, b = 2, c;` declares several fields, which share their modifiers
// and type but not their initializer - so each declarator becomes its own member.
Field       <~~ pushFields(takeAll()) ~~>
            = Mods FType FieldVar { "," FieldVar } ";" ;
FieldVar    <~~ push(makeFieldVar(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ CDims ] [ "=" VarInit ] ;

// Modifiers before a member: annotations (with or without arguments) are parsed and
// ignored; ModWord keeps the modifier text so `static` is still detectable.
Mods        <~~ push({mods: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = { Annotation | ModWord } ;
ModWord     <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = ModKw ;
FType       <~~ push({ty: up.in}) ~~>
            = Type ;

// A parameter may carry annotations, `final` and varargs (`...`); all ignored.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ Param { "," Param } ] ")" ;
// A trailing `T... name` is a VARARGS parameter: the call site collects the
// remaining arguments into an array (jFixVarargs), unless it already passes one.
Param       = { Annotation | "final" KwEnd } Type [ VarArgs ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ CDims ] ;
VarArgs     <~~ push({vararg: true}) ~~>
            = "..." ;
// The C-style declarator suffix: `int a[]` and `int[] a[]` declare arrays too.
CDims       = "[" "]" { "[" "]" } ;

// Types are parsed and (apart from field defaults) ignored. Generic arguments
// (<...>), dotted names, extra array dimensions and the extra primitive keywords are
// accepted and dropped. Semantics stay dynamic.
// A TYPE ANNOTATION may sit in front of the type, in front of any segment of a
// qualified one, and in front of each array bracket pair (`@A String @A []`,
// `Outer.@A Inner`) - JLS 9.7.4. They are dropped like every other annotation.
Type        = { Annotation } ( PrimName KwEnd | "String" KwEnd | "void" KwEnd | "var" KwEnd
              | Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." { Annotation } Id [ SkipAngle ] } ) { { Annotation } "[" "]" } ;
PrimName    = "int" | "boolean" | "char" | "long" | "double" | "float" | "short" | "byte" ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
Statement   = Block | If | While | Do | ForEach | For | Switch | Return | Yield | Break | Continue
            | Try | Throw | Assert | Synchronized | LocalClass | Labeled | Decl | Empty | ExprStmt ;
// A LOCAL class: the declaration builds it where the file is walked (so it is
// registered before the enclosing method ever runs) and leaves a do-nothing
// statement behind. Its scope is wider than Java's - it stays visible after the
// method - which no program that compiles under javac can observe.
LocalClass  <~~ push(function() {}) ~~>
            = NestedType ;
// yield: the value of the enclosing switch EXPRESSION arm. It travels as its own
// signal, so it passes out of nested blocks and loops like return does.
Yield       <~~ push(makeYield(pop())) ~~>
            = "yield" KwEnd Expression ";" ;

Block       <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;

If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf "(" Expression ")" Statement [ KwElse Statement ] ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhile(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwWhile "(" Expression ")" Statement ;

Do          <~~ push(makeDo(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwDo Statement KwWhile "(" Expression ")" ";" ;

// The classic colon switch (on int or String): execution starts at the first matching
// label and runs until a break or the end of the switch. Labels may stack.
Switch      <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSwitch "(" Expression ")" "{" { SwitchCase } "}" ;
// The arrow forms (case 1, 2 -> body) are sugar for stacked classic labels
// whose shared body ends in an implicit break: no fallthrough, several labels
// per arm. Tried first; they fail fast at ':' for the classic forms.
SwitchCase  = ArrowCase | ArrowDefault | Case | Default ;
ArrowCase   <~~ pushArrowCase(takeAll()) ~~>
            = KwCase CaseLabel { "," CaseLabel } "->" ArrowBody ;
ArrowBody   = Block | Throw | ExprStmt ;
ArrowDefault <~~ pushArrowDefault(takeAll()) ~~>
            = KwDefault "->" ArrowBody ;
Case        <~~ push(makeCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase CaseLabel ":" { Statement } ;

// ----- Case labels (shared by the statement switch and the switch expression).
// A label is a PATTERN (type or record deconstruction, with an optional `when`
// guard), `null`, a literal, a plain name, or any other constant expression.
// The specific forms come first: a bare name would otherwise be swallowed by
// Lambda ("case S -> 1" parses as the lambda "S -> 1" under a full expression),
// and a name is also the only shape an ENUM constant label has - it names no
// variable in any scope, so it is compared by its source TEXT instead.
CaseLabel   <~~ push({lab: makeLabel(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = ( NullLabel | RecPat | TypePat | LitLabel | NameLabel | ExprLabel ) [ WhenGuard ] ;
NullLabel   <~~ push({lnull: true}) ~~>
            = "null" KwEnd ;
LitLabel    <~~ push({lval: pop()}) ~~>
            = LabelLit ;
LabelLit    = TextBlock | JavaStr | CharLit | HexLit | BinLit | OctLit | FloatLit
            | UscoreInt | SuffInt | IntLit | True | False ;
NameLabel   <~~ push(makeNameLabel(takeAll(), stripWs(up.in))) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "." Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } ;
ExprLabel   <~~ push({lval: pop()}) ~~>
            = CondExpr ;
WhenGuard   <~~ push({lguard: pop()}) ~~>
            = "when" KwEnd Expression ;

// Patterns: `Type name` binds the scrutinee, `Type(sub, ...)` deconstructs a
// record component-wise (the components are matched against nested patterns).
// A `var` or primitive type in a pattern always matches - the type is erased.
Pattern     = RecPat | TypePat ;
RecPat      <~~ push(makeRecPat(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PatType "(" [ Pattern { "," Pattern } ] ")" ;
TypePat     <~~ push(makeTypePat(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PatType PatName ;
PatType     <~~ push({ptype: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = [ "final" KwEnd ] Type ;
PatName     <~~ push({pname: up.in}) ~~>
            = Id ;
Default     <~~ push(makeDefault(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDefault ":" { Statement } ;

// The enhanced for is tried first: its ':' fails fast for the classic form.
ForEach     <~~ push(makeForEach(pop(), pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwFor "(" { Annotation | "final" KwEnd } Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ CDims ] ":" Expression ")" Statement ;

For         <~~ push(makeFor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor "(" [ ForInit ] ";" [ ForCond ] ";" [ ForPost ] ")" Statement ;
ForInit     <~~ push({k:"init", t:pop()}) ~~> = ForVar | ForExpr ;
ForVar      = DeclCore ;
ForExpr     <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~> = Expression ;
ForCond     <~~ push({k:"cond", t:pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
ForPost     <~~ push({k:"post", t:exprToStmt(pop())}) ~~> = Expression ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwReturn [ Expression ] ";" ;

// break / continue accept an optional target label; a labeled form is recognized but
// not implemented (labels are dropped) and falls back to the plain innermost jump.
Break       <~~ push(makeBreakL(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>    = KwBreak [ LabelRef ] ";" ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinueL(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~> = KwContinue [ LabelRef ] ";" ;
LabelRef    <~~ push({label: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Id ;

// assert cond [: message];  recognized, not implemented (no assertion model); the
// condition and the optional message are still evaluated so their calls stay visible.
Assert      <~~ push(makeAssert(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwAssert Expression [ ":" Expression ] ";" ;
// synchronized (lock) { }: recognized, not implemented (no locks); the lock expression
// is evaluated and the block still runs.
Synchronized <~~ push(makeSync(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwSync "(" Expression ")" Block ;
// label: stmt  recognized, not implemented (the label is dropped); the statement runs.
// NotSwitchKw keeps a switch's 'default:' / 'case ...:' from being misread as a label
// (Id matches keywords in this grammar), the same guard C's Label uses.
Labeled     <~~ push(makeLabeled(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = NotSwitchKw LabelName ":" Statement ;
LabelName   <~~ push({lbl: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Id ;
NotSwitchKw = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var s = 0 // Whitespace is not skipped before a :script, so scan past it first.
        while (c.peek(s) === 32 || c.peek(s) === 9 || c.peek(s) === 10 || c.peek(s) === 13) { s++ }
        var kws = ["case", "default"]
        var hit = false
        for (var k = 0; k < kws.length; k++) {
            var w = kws[k]
            var ok = true
            for (var i = 0; i < w.length; i++) { if (c.peek(s + i) !== w.charCodeAt(i)) { ok = false } }
            if (ok) {
                var cc = c.peek(s + w.length)
                if (!((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || cc === 95 || cc === 36 || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122))) { hit = true }
            }
        }
        return hit ? abnf.newToken("\x01switch-label", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// try/catch/finally and throw are implemented (excThrow/excTry, languages/lib): throw
// raises the value, catch binds it (the first catch clause wins - exception types,
// including multi-catch A | B, are parsed but not discriminated), finally always runs.
// return/break/continue inside a try still propagate. A try-with-resources header is
// skipped (SkipParen), so its resources are not bound or auto-closed.
// try-with-resources, typed and MULTI catch clauses, finally. The clauses carry
// their declared type(s), so excTryTyped (lib/interp-core.js) can pick the first
// one that matches through core.excMatches instead of always taking the first.
Try         <~~ push(jTry(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwTry [ Resources ] TryBody { CatchClause } [ FinallyClause ] ;
// try (R a = ...; b) - each resource is closed, in REVERSE order, when the block
// ends however it ends. A bare name is an already-open effectively final resource.
Resources   <~~ push({resources: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" Resource { ";" Resource } [ ";" ] ")" ;
Resource    = ResDecl | ResRef ;
ResDecl     <~~ push(makeResDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] DeclType Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "=" Expression ;
ResRef      <~~ push(makeResRef(up.in)) ~~>
            = Id ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Block ;
CatchClause <~~ push(jCatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCatch "(" { Annotation | "final" KwEnd } CatchTypes Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ")" Block ;
CatchTypes  <~~ push({ctypes: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type { "|" Type } ;
FinallyClause <~~ push({finbody: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwFinally Block ;
Throw       <~~ push(excThrow(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow Expression ";" ;

// An explicit constructor invocation (super(args) or this(args)): accepted and
// reported as not implemented (the implicit no-argument super already ran). The
// arguments are still evaluated so their calls stay visible.
CtorCall    <~~ push(makeCtorHead(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CtorWhich "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" ";" ;
CtorWhich   <~~ push({cwhich: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = KwSuper | "this" KwEnd ;

Decl        = DeclCore ";" ;
// A local declaration may carry annotations / `final` (e.g. @SuppressWarnings("x")
// int y = 1); LeadMods drops them (they do not change the interpretation).
DeclCore    <~~ push(makeDeclList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] DeclType Declarator { "," Declarator } ;
DeclType    <~~ push({dty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
Declarator  <~~ push(makeDeclSpec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ CDims ] [ "=" VarInit ] ;
// An initializer is an expression or a BARE array initializer - `int[] a = {1, 2}`
// and the nested `int[][] j = {{1}, {2, 3}}`, which needs no `new`.
VarInit     = ArrayInit | Expression ;
ArrayInit   <~~ push(makeArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" [ VarInit { "," VarInit } [ "," ] ] "}" ;

Empty       <~~ push(function() {}) ~~>
            = ";" ;

ExprStmt    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression ";" ;

// Every expression production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value.
Expression  = Assignment | CondExpr ;
Assignment  <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "<<=" | ">>>=" | ">>=" | "&=" | "^=" | "|=" | "=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Expression ;

// An assignable place: a variable (this and class names are variables too),
// or a member/index path: this.x, p.x, Main.fails, arr[i].
Target      <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | TIndex } ;
TIndex      <~~ push({idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;

CondExpr    <~~ push(makeCond(takeAll())) ~~>
            = OrExpr [ "?" Expression ":" Expression ] ;

OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { "||" AndExpr } ;

AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = BitOrExpr { "&&" BitOrExpr } ;

// The bitwise (| ^ &) and shift (<< >> >>>) levels work on int operands with
// Java's 32-bit semantics; & | ^ on two booleans are the non-short-circuit
// boolean operators (see the binOp override below). A lone "|" that actually
// begins "||" fails its right side, the iteration rolls back, and the
// enclosing || level picks it up (same for "&" and "&&").
BitOrExpr   <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitXorExpr { "|" <~~ push("|") ~~> BitXorExpr } ;
BitXorExpr  <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitAndExpr { "^" <~~ push("^") ~~> BitAndExpr } ;
BitAndExpr  <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = EqExpr { "&" <~~ push("&") ~~> EqExpr } ;

EqExpr      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RelExpr { ( "==" | "!=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> RelExpr } ;

RelExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = InstOf { ( "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> InstOf } ;

// x instanceof Type [binding]: a genuine dynamic type test (rtIsType: numbers,
// strings, booleans, arrays, user classes via __class/__super). The optional
// pattern variable binds the operand in the enclosing scope - looser than
// Java's flow scoping, which only admits it where the test held.
InstOf      <~~ push(makeInstOf(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = ShiftExpr [ InstTail ] ;
// The record-deconstruction form (x instanceof Dot(int v)) needs the "(" to
// match, so it fails fast for the ordinary `x instanceof T [name]`.
InstTail    <~~ push(makeInstTail(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwInstanceof ( InstPat | IofType [ IofBind ] ) ;
InstPat     <~~ push({ipat: pop()}) ~~>
            = RecPat ;
IofType     <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~>
            = Type ;
IofBind     <~~ push({bind: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Id ;

ShiftExpr   <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AddExpr { ( "<<" | ">>>" | ">>" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> AddExpr } ;

AddExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = MulExpr { ( "+" | "-" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> MulExpr } ;

MulExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = UnaryExpr { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> UnaryExpr } ;

UnaryExpr   = Cast | PreIncDec | Neg | Not | BitNot | UPlus | PostfixExpr ;
// Prefix ++/-- yields the new value (genuine; must be tried before Neg so "--x" is not
// parsed as "-(-x)"). Unary + is identity. ~x is recognized but not implemented.
PreIncDec   <~~ push(makePreIncDec(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Target ;
Neg         <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" UnaryExpr ;
Not         <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "!" UnaryExpr ;
BitNot      <~~ push(makeBitNot(pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "~" UnaryExpr ;
UPlus       <~~ push(makeUPlus(pop())) ~~>
            = "+" UnaryExpr ;

// A cast is compiled as identity ((Type) expr yields expr). A primitive cast accepts
// any unary operand; a reference cast excludes a leading '-'/'+' so `(a) - b` stays
// subtraction (Java's cast/subtraction disambiguation).
Cast        = PrimCast | RefCast ;
PrimCast    <~~ var pcInner = pop(); push(makeCast(pcInner, pop().ct)) ~~>
            = "(" CastPrim ")" UnaryExpr ;
CastPrim    <~~ push({ct: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = { Annotation } PrimName KwEnd { { Annotation } "[" "]" } ;
RefCast     <~~ push(makeCast(pop(), "")) ~~>
            = "(" RefType ")" UnaryNoPM ;
UnaryNoPM   = Not | PostfixExpr ;
RefType     = { Annotation } ( "String" KwEnd | Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." { Annotation } Id [ SkipAngle ] } ) { { Annotation } "[" "]" } ;

// NewExpr is a Primary, not an alternative here, so `new Foo().bar()` and
// `new int[]{1, 2}[0]` take the ordinary suffix chain.
PostfixExpr = IncDec | SuperCall | IfaceSuper | CallMember ;
// Greet.super.hello() - the default method of a NAMED interface, which is how a
// class that inherits two defaults picks one.
IfaceSuper  <~~ push(makeIfaceSuper(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push({isup: up.in}) ~~> "." KwSuper "." Id <~~ push({ismeth: up.in}) ~~>
              "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" ;
IncDec      <~~ push(makeIncDec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// new Point(1, 2) | new int[]{1, 2, 3} | new int[n] | new Foo() { ... } (anonymous)
NewExpr     = NewAnon | NewObject | NewArrLit | NewArrSize ;
NewObject   <~~ push(makeNew(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew NewName [ SkipAngle ] "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" ;
// The type of a `new` may be qualified (new Outer.Nested()).
NewName     <~~ push(jJoinDots(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] } ;
// An anonymous class: accepted, reported as not implemented; the constructor
// arguments still run so their calls stay visible under -warn-unsupported.
// new Type(args) { members } - an anonymous subclass (of a class OR an interface),
// built once on first evaluation and instantiated like any other class.
NewAnon     <~~ push(makeNewAnon(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew NewName [ SkipAngle ] "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" AnonBody ;
AnonBody    <~~ push({anonmembers: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { Member } "}" ;
NewArrLit   = KwNew Type ArrayInit ;
// new T[a][b]...[]: every sized dimension is allocated, the trailing empty ones
// leave their elements null (new int[2][] gives two null rows).
NewArrSize  <~~ push(makeNewArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew ArrBase ArrDim { ArrDim } { ArrEmpty } ;
ArrBase     <~~ push({abase: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = PrimName KwEnd | "String" KwEnd | Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;
ArrDim      <~~ push({adim: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;
ArrEmpty    <~~ push({aempty: true}) ~~>
            = "[" "]" ;

// super.m(args): the method search starts at the superclass of the class the call
// appears in (curClassName is set while the class body is being built).
SuperCall   <~~ push(makeSuperCall(popName(), takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSuper "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" ;

// A primary followed by method calls, field reads and indexing:
// System.out.println(x), p.dist(), this.x, arr[i], Main.check(...).
CallMember  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { QNewSfx | MethodSfx | FieldSfx | IndexSfx } ;
// o.new Inner(args) - creation qualified by an enclosing instance. It is a
// SUFFIX of the member chain, not a postfix form of its own: an alternative
// beginning with a Primary would parse the qualifier once for itself and once
// again for CallMember, so deeply nested calls would cost 2^depth (the OpenJDK
// corpus has tests built to provoke exactly that). It precedes FieldSfx, which
// would otherwise read `.new` as a field named `new`.
QNewSfx     <~~ push(makeQNewSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "." KwNew Id <~~ push({qname: up.in}) ~~> [ SkipAngle ]
              "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" ;
// An explicit call-site type argument (list.<String>of(...)) is parsed and
// dropped like every other generic: types are erased in this model.
MethodSfx   <~~ push({kind:"mcall", name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "." [ SkipAngle ] Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" ;
FieldSfx    = "." Id <~~ push({kind:"field", name: up.in}) ~~> ;
IndexSfx    <~~ push({kind:"index", idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;

// A switch EXPRESSION. It comes first because it is the only Primary that starts
// with `switch`, and it has to be a Primary so `5 + switch (n) {...}` works.
SwitchExpr  <~~ push(makeSwitchExpr(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSwitch "(" Expression ")" "{" { SwitchArm } "}" ;
SwitchArm   = ArmArrow | ArmArrowDflt | ArmColon | ArmColonDflt ;
ArmArrow    <~~ push(makeArm(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = KwCase CaseLabel { "," CaseLabel } "->" ArmBody ;
ArmArrowDflt <~~ push(makeArm(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = KwDefault "->" ArmBody ;
ArmColon    <~~ push(makeArm(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = KwCase CaseLabel { "," CaseLabel } ":" ArmStmts ;
ArmColonDflt <~~ push(makeArm(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = KwDefault ":" ArmStmts ;
// An arrow body is a value, a block (which yields) or a throw; a colon body is a
// run of statements that ends in a yield.
ArmBody     = ArmBlock | ArmThrow | ArmValue ;
ArmBlock    <~~ push({sstmts: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = Block ;
ArmThrow    <~~ push({sstmts: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = Throw ;
ArmValue    <~~ push({sval: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ";" ;
ArmStmts    <~~ push({sstmts: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = { Statement } ;

Primary     = ArrClassLit | NewExpr | SwitchExpr | Lambda | MethodRef | "(" Expression ")" | TextBlock | JavaStr | CharLit
            | HexLit | BinLit | OctLit | FloatLit | UscoreInt | SuffInt | IntLit | True | False | Null
            | OuterThis | BareCall | VarRef ;

// An UNQUALIFIED method call - `check(x)` rather than `this.check(x)` or
// `Main.check(x)`. Java's own rule picks the receiver: a method the enclosing
// class (or an ancestor) declares static is called on the class descriptor,
// every other one on the enclosing instance - and a static context has none, so
// the descriptor stands in there too. Tried before VarRef, which would read the
// name alone and leave the argument list behind.
BareCall    <~~ push(makeBareCall(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" ;

// `int[].class` / `String[].class` - a class literal over an ARRAY type. The
// plain `Foo.class` needs nothing (it reads as a field), but this one is no
// member chain: `String[` starts an index that has no subscript. Class objects
// are not modelled, so the value is null. It has to beat VarRef.
ArrClassLit <~~ push(makeConst(null)) ~~>
            = ( PrimName KwEnd | Id { "." Id } ) [ SkipAngle ] "[" "]" { "[" "]" } "." "class" KwEnd ;

// Outer.this - the enclosing instance of an inner class. It has to beat VarRef,
// which would read `Outer` as the class and then look for a field named `this`.
OuterThis   <~~ push(makeOuterThis(up.in)) ~~>
            = Id "." "this" KwEnd ;

// Lambdas and method references are real closures on the runtime. A lambda captures
// the defining scope; a functional-interface call site (any method call on the
// value, whatever the single abstract method is named) invokes it. The body is an
// expression (its value is the result) or a block (a return supplies the result).
Lambda      <~~ push(makeLambdaExpr(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = LambdaHead "->" LambdaBody ;
LambdaHead  = "(" [ LamParam { "," LamParam } ] ")" | LamId ;
// A parameter is an optional type then a name; the explicit "Type LamId | LamId"
// alternation lets an untyped "(x)" work despite the possessive optional (Type
// would otherwise greedily consume the sole identifier).
LamParam    = [ "final" KwEnd ] ( Type LamId | LamId ) ;
LamId       <~~ push({lparam: up.in}) ~~> = Id ;
LambdaBody  = LamBlock | LamExpr ;
LamBlock    <~~ push({lblock: pop()}) ~~> = Block ;
LamExpr     <~~ push({lexpr: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
// A method reference Recv::m becomes a closure that forwards its arguments to
// Recv.m(...). Constructor references (::new) stay not implemented.
MethodRef   <~~ push(makeMethodRef(stripWs(up.in), up.pos)) ~~>
            = Id { "." Id } "::" ( Id | KwNew ) ;

// Java's string literal. Same shape as lib/dq-strings.abnf, but decoded by
// jUnescape, which knows Java's octal escapes ("A\102") and \s.
JavaStr     = '"' :whitespace() { StrEsc | DqPlain } <~~ push(makeConst(jUnescape(up.in))) ~~> '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

Null        <~~ push(makeConst(null)) ~~>  = "null" KwEnd ;

// 'this' and class names resolve like variables.
VarRef      <~~ push(makeVarRef(up.in)) ~~>
            = Id ;

// ----- Widened literals. The numeric forms are genuine now: hex / binary /
// octal / underscored / suffixed integers and fractional / exponent / hex-float
// doubles all evaluate to their value (one number type; an L/f/D suffix only
// selects the parse - see numLit). Char literals and text blocks stay
// recognized-but-not-implemented. Each form matches something plain IntLit
// cannot, so ordinary decimal integers still reach IntLit untouched. -----

// A char literal. Java's char is an integral type that PRINTS as a glyph, so the
// value is a boxed {__char: code}: every operator unboxes it (so 'b' - 'a' == 1 and
// c == 65 hold) while string context renders the character. The escape set is
// Java's: the single letters, a one-to-three digit OCTAL escape and \u form.
CharLit     <~~ push(makeConst(jChar(jCharCode(up.in)))) ~~>
            = "'" :whitespace() CharBody "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CharBody    = "\\" CharEscBody | CharPlain ;
CharEscBody = "u" JHexD JHexD JHexD JHexD | JOctRun | CharEsc ;
JOctRun     = JOct [ JOct [ JOct ] ] ;
JOct        = "0"..."7" ;
JHexD       = "0"..."9" | "a"..."f" | "A"..."F" ;
CharEsc     = "n" | "t" | "r" | "b" | "f" | "s" | "'" | '"' | "\\" ;
CharPlain   = "\x09" | "\x0b"..."\x0c" | "\x20"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// A text block """ ... """ ; the !'"""' lookahead lets single and double quotes appear
// inside without ending the block. Whitespace is disabled inside so newlines are content.
TextBlock   <~~ push(makeConst(jTextBlock(up.in))) ~~>
            = '"""' :whitespace() { !'"""' TBItem } '"""' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
TBItem      = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" | "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// Hex / binary: the 0x / 0b prefix is distinctive, so these never shadow a decimal int.
// A hex literal may carry a p-exponent (0x1p4 == 16.0, a hexadecimal double).
HexLit      <~~ push(makeConst(numLit(up.in, 16))) ~~>
            = ( "0x" | "0X" ) :whitespace() HexDig { HexDig | "_" } [ HexPTail ] [ NumSfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HexPTail    = ( "p" | "P" ) [ "+" | "-" ] Dig { Dig } ;
BinLit      <~~ push(makeConst(numLit(up.in, 2))) ~~>
            = ( "0b" | "0B" ) :whitespace() BinDig { BinDig | "_" } [ NumSfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// The classic leading-zero octal (017); it needs at least one octal digit after
// the 0, so plain 0, 0.5 and 0x... are untouched.
OctLit      <~~ push(makeConst(numLit(up.in, 8))) ~~>
            = "0" :whitespace() OctDig { OctDig | "_" } [ NumSfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A fractional or exponent literal: 1.5, 5., .5, 1e3, 2.5e-2 (underscores and
// an f/F/d/D/L suffix allowed). Distinct from an integer and from x.y access.
FloatLit    <~~ push(makeConst(numLit(up.in, 10))) ~~>
            = FloatBody [ NumSfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
FloatBody   = "." :whitespace() Dig { Dig | "_" } [ ExpPart ]
            | Dig :whitespace() { Dig | "_" } ( "." { Dig | "_" } [ ExpPart ] | ExpPart ) ;
ExpPart     = ( "e" | "E" ) [ "+" | "-" ] Dig { Dig } ;
// A decimal digit run containing an underscore (a digit-grouping literal like 1_000_000).
UscoreInt   <~~ push(makeConst(numLit(up.in, 10))) ~~>
            = Dig :whitespace() { Dig } "_" { Dig | "_" } [ NumSfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A decimal digit run with an L / F / D suffix (like 100L, 2F, 5D).
SuffInt     <~~ push(makeConst(numLit(up.in, 10))) ~~>
            = Dig :whitespace() { Dig } NumSfx :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HexDig      = "0"..."9" | "a"..."f" | "A"..."F" ;
BinDig      = "0" | "1" ;
OctDig      = "0"..."7" ;
Dig         = "0"..."9" ;
NumSfx      = "L" | "l" | "F" | "f" | "D" | "d" ;

// ----- Widened surface: generics, annotations, exceptions, casts (see :description) -----

// Balanced skips let a construct be ACCEPTED without being understood. String and char
// literals are matched first so braces/brackets inside them are never miscounted. No
// tags: nothing is pushed, so the consuming production places its own placeholder.
SkipBlock   = "{" { SkipItem } "}" ;
SkipItem    = SkipStr | SkipChar | SkipBlock | SkipOther ;
SkipStr     = '"' :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipStrChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipStrEsc  = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipStrChar = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipChar    = "'" :whitespace() { SkipChEsc | SkipChChar } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipChEsc   = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipChChar  = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipOther   = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A balanced < ... > run (generic parameters / arguments), parsed and ignored.
SkipAngle   = "<" { SkipAngle | SkipAngleItem } ">" ;
SkipAngleItem = "\x00"..."\x3b" | "\x3d" | "\x3f"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A balanced ( ... ) run (annotation arguments), parsed and ignored.
SkipParen   = "(" { SkipStr | SkipChar | SkipParen | SkipPOther } ")" ;
SkipPOther  = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x2a"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// Annotations (with or without arguments), modifier keywords, and the implements /
// throws clauses are parsed and ignored. LeadMods drops everything (headers with no
// static to track). ModWord (used by Mods) keeps the modifier text.
// `@interface` opens an annotation TYPE, so it is never an annotation - without
// the guard, LeadMods would eat it and the declaration behind it would not parse
// (`@Retention(...) public @interface A {}` is the ordinary shape). NotIfaceKw is
// a pure function of (source, position), like NotSwitchKw above.
Annotation  = "@" NotIfaceKw Id { "." Id } [ SkipParen ] ;
NotIfaceKw  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var s = 0 // Whitespace is not skipped before a :script, so scan past it first.
        while (c.peek(s) === 32 || c.peek(s) === 9 || c.peek(s) === 10 || c.peek(s) === 13) { s++ }
        var w = "interface"
        var ok = true
        for (var i = 0; i < w.length; i++) { if (c.peek(s + i) !== w.charCodeAt(i)) { ok = false } }
        if (ok) {
            var cc = c.peek(s + w.length)
            if (!((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || cc === 95 || cc === 36 || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122))) {
                return abnf.newToken("\x01annotation-type", 0)
            }
        }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;
ModKw       = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" | "static" | "final" | "abstract"
              | "synchronized" | "native" | "transient" | "volatile" | "strictfp"
              | "non-sealed" | "sealed" | "default" ) KwEnd ;
LeadMods    = { Annotation | ModKw } ;
ImplementsSkip = KwImplements ImplRef { "," ImplRef } ;
ImplRef     <~~ push({impl: jJoinDots(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] } ;
Throws      = KwThrows Type { "," Type } ;
CatchType   = Type { "|" Type } ;

// Type declarations accepted structurally, then reported as not implemented (so a
// whole Java file parses; -warn-unsupported lets the rest run). Bodies are skipped.
UnsupType   = AnnotationDecl | Interface | EnumDecl ;
// An interface: constants (implicitly static final), abstract methods, `default`
// and `private` instance methods and `static` methods. `sealed ... permits` is
// accepted and dropped - the hierarchy is not checked, only parsed.
Permits     = "permits" KwEnd Type { "," Type } ;
IfaceExt    <~~ push({iext: jJoinDots(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] } ;
// An annotation TYPE is pure metadata - nothing in this model reads it at run
// time - so the declaration is parsed and dropped, like the annotations themselves.
AnnotationDecl = LeadMods "@" KwInterface Id SkipBlock ;
Interface   <~~ buildInterface(takeAll()) ~~>
            = LeadMods KwInterface Id <~~ push(up.in); jEnterClass(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ]
              [ KwExtends IfaceExt { "," IfaceExt } ] [ Permits ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// enum E { A(1), B { ... }; members }: a class whose constants are singleton
// instances built once, in order. A constant with a body becomes an anonymous
// SUBCLASS of the enum (so it can override a method), which is why the class
// descriptor is built first and the constants are instantiated afterwards.
EnumDecl    <~~ buildEnum(takeAll()) ~~>
            = LeadMods KwEnum Id <~~ push(up.in); jEnterClass(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] [ ImplementsSkip ]
              "{" [ EnumConst { "," EnumConst } ] [ "," ] [ ";" { Member } ] "}" ;
EnumConst   <~~ push(makeEnumConst(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { Annotation } Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ EnumArgs ] [ EnumBody ] ;
EnumArgs    <~~ push({eargs: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" ;
EnumBody    <~~ push({emembers: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { Member } "}" ;

// A type declared inside a class body (nested, inner or a local class in a
// method body) is the ordinary declaration again: its tag builds it exactly
// like a top-level one, in the order the file is walked, so it is registered
// before anything can name it. It contributes NO member to the enclosing type
// (the build tags push nothing), and it is additionally registered under its
// qualified name, so `new Outer.Nested()` resolves.
NestedType  = ClassDecl | RecordDecl | Interface | EnumDecl | AnnotationDecl ;

KwClass     = "class" KwEnd ;
KwRecord    = "record" KwEnd ;
KwImplements = "implements" KwEnd ;
KwInterface = "interface" KwEnd ;
KwEnum      = "enum" KwEnd ;
KwThrows    = "throws" KwEnd ;
KwTry       = "try" KwEnd ;
KwCatch     = "catch" KwEnd ;
KwFinally   = "finally" KwEnd ;
KwThrow     = "throw" KwEnd ;
KwPackage   = "package" KwEnd ;
KwImport    = "import" KwEnd ;
KwIf        = "if" KwEnd ;
KwElse      = "else" KwEnd ;
KwWhile     = "while" KwEnd ;
KwDo        = "do" KwEnd ;
KwFor       = "for" KwEnd ;
KwReturn    = "return" KwEnd ;
KwBreak     = "break" KwEnd ;
KwContinue  = "continue" KwEnd ;
KwNew       = "new" KwEnd ;
KwExtends   = "extends" KwEnd ;
KwSuper     = "super" KwEnd ;
KwSwitch    = "switch" KwEnd ;
KwCase      = "case" KwEnd ;
KwDefault   = "default" KwEnd ;
KwInstanceof = "instanceof" KwEnd ;
KwAssert    = "assert" KwEnd ;
KwSync      = "synchronized" KwEnd ;

// ---


:startScript(~~

    // Shared interpreter machinery (scopes, BRK/CONT protocol, expression and
    // statement builders); language-specific behavior is configured on `core` below.
    include("lib/interp-core.js")

    // The host names a program can see. System and Math are built here.
    hostGlobals["System"] = {
        out: {
            // println() with no argument writes just the line terminator; without
            // the arity test the missing parameter rendered as "null".
            println: function(x) { println(arguments.length == 0 ? "" : jstr(x)) },
            print: function(x) { print(jstr(x)) }
        },
        exit: function(code) { exit(code | 0) }
    }
    // Math.abs/max/min keep the argument type: a double in, a double out (Java has
    // an int and a double overload of each).
    hostGlobals["Math"] = {
        abs: function(v) {
            if (jIsFlo(v)) { var f = v.__flo; return jFlo(f < 0 ? jNegF(f) : f) }
            return v < 0 ? -v | 0 : v | 0
        },
        max: function(a, b) { return jMinMax(a, b, jNumD(a) > jNumD(b)) },
        min: function(a, b) { return jMinMax(a, b, jNumD(a) < jNumD(b)) }
    }

    // ----- char: a boxed {__char: code} value -----
    // Java's char is an integral type that renders as a glyph, so it can be neither a
    // plain number (then "" + c would print 98) nor a one-character string (then
    // c == 65 would be false). Boxing keeps both: binOp unboxes for EVERY operator,
    // jstr renders the character, and only a (char) cast or String.charAt boxes again.
    function jChar(code) { return {__char: code} }
    function jIsChar(v) { return v !== null && v !== undefined && typeof v == "object" && v.__char !== undefined }
    function jNum(v) { return jIsChar(v) ? v.__char : (jIsFlo(v) ? v.__flo : v) }

    // ----- double / float: a boxed {__flo: value} -----
    // Java's `double` is a DIFFERENT type from `int`, and the value model here is
    // untyped, so 1.0 and 1 would be the same JS number: 1.0 / 3.0 would divide as
    // integers (0 instead of 0.3333333333333333) and println(1.0) would print "1"
    // instead of "1.0". Boxing every floating-point value keeps the two apart, the
    // same way jChar keeps a char apart from its code and the same way the compiled
    // world's js_jflo box does (see abnf/jsrtjvm.go). Invariant: a PLAIN number is an
    // integral type (int/long/short/byte), a {__flo} box is a double/float.
    var jNegOne = 0 - 1
    var jZeroF = 0
    var jInf = 1 / jZeroF
    function jFlo(f) { return {__flo: f} }
    // Math.max/min pick an operand, but the DOUBLE overload is selected as soon as
    // one side is a double, so the answer is a double then (Math.max(1.5, 2) is 2.0).
    function jMinMax(a, b, takeA) {
        var v = anytype
        v = takeA ? a : b
        if (jIsFlo(a) || jIsFlo(b)) { return jIsFlo(v) ? v : jFlo(jNumD(v)) }
        return v
    }
    function jIsFlo(v) { return v !== null && v !== undefined && typeof v == "object" && v.__flo !== undefined }
    // The numeric value of any numeric operand (a plain number, a char or a double).
    function jNumD(v) {
        if (v === null || v === undefined) return v
        if (typeof v == "object") {
            if (v.__flo !== undefined) return v.__flo
            if (v.__char !== undefined) return v.__char
        }
        return v
    }
    function jNegF(f) { return f * jNegOne }
    // One binary operator with at least one double operand: the whole operation is
    // floating point (JLS 5.6.2, binary numeric promotion) and its result is a double.
    function jFloOp(op, l, r) {
        if (op == "+" && (typeof l == "string" || typeof r == "string")) { return jstr2(l) + jstr2(r) }
        var a = jNumD(l)
        var b = jNumD(r)
        if (op == "+")  return jFlo(a + b)
        if (op == "-")  return jFlo(a - b)
        if (op == "*")  return jFlo(a * b)
        if (op == "/")  return jFlo(a / b)
        if (op == "%")  return jFlo(a % b)
        if (op == "==") return a == b
        if (op == "!=") return a != b
        if (op == "<")  return a < b
        if (op == ">")  return a > b
        if (op == "<=") return a <= b
        if (op == ">=") return a >= b
        return coreBinOp(op, a, b)
    }
    // Double.toString (JLS 5.1.11 / java.lang.Double): always a decimal point, the
    // shortest digit run that round-trips, plain decimal for 1e-3 <= |d| < 1e7 and
    // computerized scientific notation ("1.0E20") outside it.
    function jFloStr(d) {
        if (d !== d) return "NaN"
        if (d === jInf) return "Infinity"
        if (d === jNegF(jInf)) return "-Infinity"
        if (d === 0) return (1 / d < 0) ? "-0.0" : "0.0"
        var neg = d < 0
        var a = neg ? jNegF(d) : d
        var s = (a >= 0.001 && a < 10000000) ? jFloPlain(a) : jFloSci(a)
        return neg ? "-" + s : s
    }
    function jFloPlain(a) {
        var s = "" + a
        if (s.indexOf(".") < 0) { s = s + ".0" }
        return s
    }
    // Re-renders the host's shortest decimal for a as Java's d.dddEnn. The host
    // switches to exponent form at different magnitudes than Java does, so the
    // digits are recovered first and the form is rebuilt.
    function jFloSci(a) {
        var s = "" + a
        var mant = s
        var exp = 0
        var ei = 0 - 1
        for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) { if (s.charAt(i) == "e" || s.charAt(i) == "E") { ei = i } }
        if (ei >= 0) {
